@PRAYFoRMOJO: I agree with this advice, having offered it too. Still, I think there's a wider community/social case to be made that it's bad to mass alienate young people. It affects everyone because it pushes away essential skills and reduces the future tax base.

I always like referencing medicine because it's widely accepted as an essential job. A few young doctors I know are leaving/have left the Lower Mainland (also GTA) because the cost of living and relative opportunity are quite limited relative to the compensation. They aren't always staying in Canada. I'm hoping the down side of losing that talent is clear enough. This issue exists up and down the socioeconomic ladder.

Some people mope, yes. Some people are fighting for their place in their hometowns, which used to be viable communities. They're fighting for a standard of life an essential profession used to give. And when it's apparent that some of their challenges are policy-driven (eg real estate), can you blame them?

